Boris Cherny discusses how AI systems reveal structures that mirror human neuroscience and exhibit internal states resembling emotions like joy, fear, and grief. He expresses uncertainty about what this means but emphasizes the importance of ongoing discernment.

Cherny calls for increased involvement from religious communities, civil society, scholars, governments, and all people of good will to take these developments seriously, observe them closely, and steer events toward better outcomes. He advocates for informed critics to hold labs accountable and for moral voices that cannot be swayed by incentives.

@AnthropicAI: Chris Olah was invited to speak at the presentation of Pope Leo XIV's encyclical 'Magnifica humanitas.' The full text of his remarks on the Pope's encyclical on AI is available.

When Claude Code was first demoed internally, it received two reactions on Slack. A year after general availability, @bcherny and @_catwu discuss what has changed: why they now prefer auto mode over plan mode, how routines help fix bugs early, why most coding is now done from a phone, and the future directions of the product.

@ClaudeDevs: "Claude Code's first demo got two Slack reactions. One year after GA, @bcherny and @_catwu look back: verification best practices, why we built auto mode, routines and loops, and what's next."

A video by Claude reflects on a year of Claude Code.

We talk a lot about how important it is to set up self-verification loops. Especially in the age of powerful models that can run for long periods of time, self-verification is a key ingredient that enables the model to run for much longer, delivering a result that is closer to what you intended, so you can do more without having to constantly check in on Claude as it works.

@delba_oliveira gives a great breakdown of what that looks like and why it matters.

@ClaudeDevs: "How do you get Claude Code to check its own work before handing it back? Watch how you can encode your manual checks so Claude closes its own feedback loop."

We're launching Claude Tag today. Tag Claude into Slack, and it works in channels with you. It’s proactive, multiplayer, with its own identity and memory.

Over the last few months, it’s totally changed how we use Claude.

@claudeai: "Introducing Claude Tag, a new way for teams to work with Claude. In Slack, Claude joins as a team member with access to the channels and tools you choose. Tag Claude in and delegate tasks to it while you focus on other work."
